LESSONS = [
{"code": "Welcome", "sem": "", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Undergrad Recap: The Lectures", "sub": "B.S. Computer Engineering · Cal State Fullerton · 2019–2021",
  "say": "Welcome back to class. Over the next hour or so, we're going to walk through your entire computer engineering degree, one course at a time, in the order you took them. But this isn't a summary. For every course, I'm going to teach you the core ideas again, the way a professor would on the first day of review week: the key concepts, the intuition behind them, and worked examples you can follow along with. Where your own labs and projects connect to the material, I'll point that out, because you've already done more of this than you probably remember. Grab a coffee. Let's start in Spring of twenty nineteen."},
]},

{"code": "EGCP 281", "sem": "Spring 2019", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Designing with VHDL", "sub": "EGCP 281 · Dr. Mike Turi",
  "say": "Our first course is E G C P two eighty one, Designing with V H D L. This is where you stopped drawing logic gates by hand and started describing hardware with code. And the most important idea in this whole course is a mindset shift, so let's start there."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Code that becomes a circuit", "pts": ["Every concurrent statement is a piece of hardware", "All of it runs at the same time — always", "Ask: what gates or flip-flops will this line become?", "entity = the pins · architecture = the inside"],
  "say": "When you write software, lines run one after another. In V H D L, every concurrent statement describes a piece of circuitry, and all of those pieces exist at the same time, reacting the instant their inputs change. So here's the rule I want you to carry forever: before you write a line of H D L, picture the gates or flip-flops it will turn into. If you can't picture it, you don't understand the line yet. A design has two halves. The entity lists the ports, like the pins on a chip. The architecture describes what's inside. And bigger designs are built by dropping smaller ones in as components and wiring them up with port maps, exactly like drawing a schematic."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Example: a full adder", "code": "s    <= a xor b xor cin;\ncout <= (a and b) or (cin and (a xor b));\n\n-- try it:  a=1, b=1, cin=0\n--   s    = 1 xor 1 xor 0 = 0\n--   cout = (1) or (0)     = 1   ->  binary 10 = 2",
  "say": "Let's build one. A full adder adds three bits: a, b, and a carry in. The sum bit is a X O R b X O R carry in. The carry out is a AND b, OR carry in AND the X O R of a and b. Let's test it in our heads. Take a equals one, b equals one, carry in equals zero. One X O R one is zero, X O R zero is still zero, so the sum is zero. For the carry, a AND b is one, so carry out is one. Reading carry then sum, we get one zero in binary, which is two. And one plus one is two. Now, chain four of these together, feeding each carry out into the next carry in, and you've built a four bit ripple carry adder. That's exactly your Lab 2."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Processes, clocks and the latch trap", "pts": ["if rising_edge(clk)  →  flip-flops", "Combinational process: list every input, assign every output", "Miss a branch → synthesis infers a latch (bug!)", "Max clock ≈ 1 / (t_clk→q + t_logic + t_setup)"],
  "say": "For sequential logic we use a process. Inside a process, statements look sequential, but they still synthesize to hardware. If you wrap your logic in if rising edge of clock, every signal you assign becomes a flip-flop. Now here's the classic beginner bug. If you write a combinational process, say a multiplexer with an if statement, and you forget to assign the output in one of the branches, the synthesizer has to remember the old value. And remembering means memory, so it quietly builds a latch you never asked for. The fix is simple: give every output a default value at the top of the process. Last idea: timing. A signal has to leave a flip-flop, travel through your logic, and arrive before the next clock edge with some setup time to spare. So your maximum clock frequency is one over the sum of clock to Q delay, logic delay, and setup time. If your logic is too deep, your clock has to slow down."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Finite state machines", "pts": ["State register + next-state logic + output logic", "Moore: outputs depend on state only (clean, a cycle later)", "Mealy: outputs depend on state AND inputs (faster, can glitch)", "Example: detect the input sequence 1-0-1"],
  "say": "Finally, state machines, which you'll see in almost every course after this one. A state machine has three parts: a register that holds the current state, logic that computes the next state, and logic that computes the outputs. In a Moore machine, outputs depend only on the state. In a Mealy machine, they depend on the state and the current inputs. Quick example: detect the pattern one, zero, one on a serial input. Start in an idle state. See a one, move to state got one. Then see a zero, move to got one zero. Then see a one, and you've found the pattern, so you raise the output. In Moore style, that needs a fourth state called found. In Mealy style, you can raise the output right on that transition, one state fewer and a cycle sooner. That trade-off, simplicity versus speed, shows up over and over in engineering."},
]},

{"code": "PHYS 227", "sem": "Spring 2019", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Waves, Optics & Modern Physics", "sub": "PHYS 227",
  "say": "Also in Spring twenty nineteen, you took Physics two twenty seven. Your folder only kept a WileyPLUS receipt, so let's rebuild the whole course from scratch. It's the physics of things that wiggle."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Oscillations and waves", "pts": ["Restoring force F = −kx  →  x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ)", "Spring: ω = √(k/m) · Pendulum: ω = √(g/L)", "Traveling wave: v = f λ", "Standing waves on a string: λₙ = 2L / n"],
  "say": "Anything with a restoring force proportional to how far you pull it, like a spring with force equals minus k x, oscillates as a perfect cosine. For a spring, the angular frequency is the square root of k over m. Stiffer spring, faster wiggle. Heavier mass, slower wiggle. For a pendulum it's the square root of g over L, which is why a grandfather clock's period depends only on its length. A traveling wave obeys the simplest formula in physics: speed equals frequency times wavelength. Example: sound at three hundred forty meters per second with a frequency of four hundred forty hertz, that's the note A, has a wavelength of three forty divided by four forty, about seventy seven centimeters. And on a guitar string fixed at both ends, only wavelengths of two L over n fit, which is why a string has a fundamental plus harmonics."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Light: refraction and interference", "pts": ["Snell: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂", "Total internal reflection: sin θc = n₂ / n₁", "Double slit bright fringes: d sin θ = m λ", "Photons: E = h f · photoelectric: K = hf − φ"],
  "say": "Light bends when it changes medium, by Snell's law: n one sine theta one equals n two sine theta two. Go from glass, n about one point five, into air, n equals one, and beyond a critical angle the light can't get out at all. Sine of the critical angle is one over one point five, so about forty two degrees. That's total internal reflection, and it's how fiber optic cables trap light for kilometers. Light is also a wave, so it interferes: two slits a distance d apart give bright fringes wherever d sine theta equals a whole number of wavelengths. And yet light is also a particle. Each photon carries energy h times f. Shine light on a metal and electrons pop out only if h f beats the metal's work function, no matter how bright the light is. That single experiment launched quantum mechanics, and quantum energy bands are what make semiconductors, and therefore your whole degree, possible."},
]},

{"code": "EGEE 203", "sem": "Fall 2019", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Electric Circuits", "sub": "EGEE 203 + 203L · Dr. Tehrani · Prof. Hilfi",
  "say": "Fall twenty nineteen. Electric Circuits, E G E E two oh three. This is the foundation of everything electrical. Three laws, and a toolbox of tricks for applying them."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "The three laws", "pts": ["Ohm: V = I R", "KCL: currents into a node sum to zero (charge conserved)", "KVL: voltages around a loop sum to zero (energy conserved)", "Divider: Vx = V · Rx / Rtotal"],
  "say": "Ohm's law: voltage equals current times resistance. Kirchhoff's current law: whatever current flows into a node must flow out, because charge doesn't pile up. Kirchhoff's voltage law: walk around any closed loop and the voltage rises and drops add to zero, because energy is conserved. Every technique in this course is just an organized way of applying those three. The most useful shortcut is the voltage divider. Twelve volts across two resistors in series, four kilo-ohms and eight kilo-ohms. The voltage across the eight k is twelve times eight over twelve, which is eight volts. You'll do that calculation in your head for the rest of your career."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Worked example: Thevenin equivalent", "code": "  12 V source ── R1 = 4 kΩ ──┬── a\n                             │\n                         R2 = 12 kΩ\n                             │\n  ground ────────────────────┴── b\n\nVth = 12 · 12/(4+12) = 9 V\nRth = 4k ∥ 12k = (4·12)/(4+12) = 3 kΩ",
  "say": "Here's the most powerful idea in the course. Any linear circuit, no matter how messy, looks from two terminals like a single voltage source in series with a single resistor. That's the Thevenin equivalent. Let's find it for this circuit: a twelve volt source, a four k resistor in series, and a twelve k resistor across the output terminals a and b. Step one, the Thevenin voltage is the open circuit voltage at a b. That's just a divider: twelve times twelve over sixteen, which is nine volts. Step two, the Thevenin resistance. Turn off the source, which means replacing the voltage source with a wire, and look back into the terminals. The four k and twelve k are now in parallel: four times twelve over sixteen is three k. So this whole circuit behaves like a nine volt battery with three k of internal resistance. Now you can hook up any load and get the answer instantly. Bonus fact: maximum power goes to the load when the load resistance equals the Thevenin resistance, three k here."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Capacitors, inductors and transients", "pts": ["Capacitor: i = C dv/dt — voltage can't jump", "Inductor: v = L di/dt — current can't jump", "x(t) = x(∞) + [x(0) − x(∞)] e^(−t/τ)", "τ = RC or L/R · ~5τ to settle"],
  "say": "Capacitors and inductors add time to the story. A capacitor's voltage can't change instantly. An inductor's current can't change instantly. Any circuit with one of them follows the same recipe: final value, plus initial value minus final value, times e to the minus t over tau. Example: a ten k resistor charging a one hundred microfarad capacitor from a five volt step. Tau is R C, ten thousand times one hundred micro, which is one second. After one second the capacitor is at sixty three percent, about three point two volts. After five seconds, it's essentially at five volts. Find three things: initial, final, and tau, and you've solved every first order circuit."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "AC circuits with phasors", "pts": ["V_m cos(ωt + φ)  →  V_m ∠ φ", "Z_R = R · Z_L = jωL · Z_C = 1 / (jωC)", "All DC methods now work with complex numbers", "Power: P = V_rms I_rms cos θ"],
  "say": "For sinusoidal sources, we use the phasor trick. A cosine at a single frequency is completely described by its amplitude and phase, so we write it as a complex number. Then resistors, inductors and capacitors all become impedances. A resistor is R. An inductor is j omega L. A capacitor is one over j omega C. Suddenly differential equations turn into algebra, and nodal analysis, dividers and Thevenin all work exactly as before, just with complex numbers. Example: a one hundred millihenry inductor at sixty hertz. Omega is two pi times sixty, about three seventy seven. Times point one is about thirty seven point seven ohms of reactance. That's how you analyze the AC circuits from your Lab 8, and how the entire power grid is analyzed."},
]},

{"code": "PHYS 226", "sem": "Fall 2019", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Electricity & Magnetism", "sub": "PHYS 226 · Michael Dubuque",
  "say": "Same semester, Physics two twenty six, electricity and magnetism. Circuits class gave you the rules. This class explains where those rules come from."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Charges, fields and potential", "pts": ["Coulomb: F = k q₁q₂ / r²,  k ≈ 9×10⁹", "Field: E = F / q  (force per unit charge)", "Gauss: ∯E·dA = Q_enc / ε₀", "Capacitor: C = ε₀A/d,  U = ½CV²"],
  "say": "Two charges push or pull with a force of k q one q two over r squared, where k is about nine times ten to the ninth. Double the distance and the force drops to a quarter. The electric field is just that force per unit of test charge, a map of the push a charge would feel at every point. Gauss's law says the total field poking out through a closed surface equals the enclosed charge over epsilon naught. With symmetry, that turns hard integrals into one line. For example, it tells you the field inside a conductor is zero, which is why a car is a safe place in a lightning storm. And a parallel plate capacitor has C equals epsilon naught A over d: bigger plates and a smaller gap store more charge. The energy stored is one half C V squared, the same formula you used in circuits."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Magnetism and induction", "pts": ["Force on moving charge: F = q v × B", "Long wire: B = μ₀ I / (2π r)", "Faraday: emf = −dΦ/dt  (Lenz: opposes the change)", "Transformer: V₂ / V₁ = N₂ / N₁"],
  "say": "A magnetic field pushes on a moving charge sideways, perpendicular to both its velocity and the field. Because the push is always sideways, it bends the path into a circle but never speeds the charge up. Put current through a wire loop in a magnetic field and you get a torque, and that's every electric motor ever built. Now flip it around. Faraday's law says a changing magnetic flux through a loop induces a voltage, and Lenz's law says the induced current fights the change. Move a magnet toward a coil and the coil pushes back. This is how generators, wireless chargers and transformers work. A transformer with one hundred turns on the primary and ten on the secondary steps one hundred twenty volts down to twelve. Put all of these laws together with Maxwell's correction and out pops a wave moving at the speed of light, which tells you light is an electromagnetic wave."},
]},

{"code": "MATH 250B", "sem": "Fall 2019", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Linear Algebra & Differential Equations", "sub": "MATH 250B · Chris Lyons",
  "say": "Math two fifty B. Two subjects that turn out to be one subject: differential equations and linear algebra. Let's start with equations that describe change."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "First-order ODE: a cooling cup of coffee", "code": "dT/dt = −k (T − 20)        T(0) = 90 °C,  k = 0.1 /min\n\nseparate:  dT/(T − 20) = −k dt\nintegrate: ln|T − 20| = −k t + C\nsolution:  T(t) = 20 + 70 e^(−0.1 t)\n\nT(10) = 20 + 70 e^(−1) ≈ 45.8 °C",
  "say": "Newton's law of cooling says a coffee cools at a rate proportional to how much hotter it is than the room. The room is twenty degrees, the coffee starts at ninety, and k is point one per minute. This equation is separable: move all the T's to one side and the t's to the other, integrate, and you get the natural log of T minus twenty equals minus k t plus a constant. Exponentiate, apply the initial condition, and T of t is twenty plus seventy e to the minus point one t. After ten minutes, that's twenty plus seventy over e, about forty six degrees. Notice the shape: it's exactly the same exponential as charging a capacitor. Nature reuses the same equation everywhere."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Linear algebra: Ax = b", "pts": ["Row-reduce [A | b]: one, infinitely many, or no solutions", "A invertible ⇔ det A ≠ 0 ⇔ columns independent", "Basis = independent + spanning · dimension = # in basis", "rank + nullity = number of columns"],
  "say": "Linear algebra is the study of systems like A x equals b, and nodal analysis from circuits produces exactly these. Row reduce the augmented matrix and you find out whether there's one solution, infinitely many, or none. The invertible matrix theorem ties a dozen facts together: A is invertible exactly when its determinant isn't zero, exactly when its columns are independent, exactly when A x equals zero has only the zero solution. Then we generalize to vector spaces: any set you can add and scale, including polynomials and solutions of differential equations. A basis is a minimal set that builds everything, and the number of vectors in it is the dimension."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Eigenvalues: the directions a matrix only stretches", "code": "A = [ 2  1 ]        det(A − λI) = (2−λ)² − 1 = 0\n    [ 1  2 ]        λ = 1  or  λ = 3\n\nλ = 3:  v = [1, 1]     A·[1,1] = [3,3]  ✓\nλ = 1:  v = [1,−1]     A·[1,−1] = [1,−1] ✓",
  "say": "Eigenvalues are the payoff of the course. An eigenvector is a direction that a matrix only stretches, never rotates, and the eigenvalue is the stretch factor. Take the matrix two, one, one, two. Set the determinant of A minus lambda I to zero: two minus lambda squared minus one equals zero, so lambda is one or three. For lambda equals three, the eigenvector is one, one. Check: A times one, one gives three, three. For lambda equals one, it's one, minus one. Now the connection. A system of differential equations x prime equals A x has solutions that grow like e to the lambda t. Negative eigenvalues mean everything decays, so the system is stable. Positive means it blows up. That's the exact idea you'll meet again as poles in signals and systems."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Second-order ODEs: springs and RLC circuits", "pts": ["a y″ + b y′ + c y = 0  →  try y = e^(rt)", "Real roots: overdamped · repeated: critical", "Complex α ± iβ: e^(αt)(c₁cos βt + c₂sin βt)", "y = y_homogeneous + y_particular"],
  "say": "Last topic. A mass on a spring with friction, or a resistor, inductor and capacitor in series, both obey a y double prime plus b y prime plus c y equals zero. Guess y equals e to the r t, and you get a quadratic, the characteristic equation. Two real roots, the system creeps back without oscillating: overdamped. A double root is critically damped, the fastest return with no overshoot, which is what car suspensions aim for. Complex roots, alpha plus or minus i beta, give a decaying oscillation: it rings at frequency beta while dying off at rate alpha. Add a forcing term and the full answer is the homogeneous solution plus one particular solution. That's the whole story of second order systems."},
]},

{"code": "CPSC 351", "sem": "Fall 2019", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Operating Systems", "sub": "CPSC 351 · Prof. Thomas Bettens",
  "say": "Operating Systems, C P S C three fifty one. The program that runs all the other programs. We'll cover processes, threads, synchronization, and memory, with examples."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Processes and threads", "pts": ["Process = running program + its own address space (PCB)", "fork() clones · exec() replaces · wait() reaps", "Threads share code, heap, files — own stack + registers", "Amdahl: speedup ≤ 1 / (S + (1 − S)/N)"],
  "say": "A process is a program in execution, with its own private memory, and the operating system tracks it in a process control block. On Unix, fork makes a copy of the running process. It returns zero in the child and the child's I D in the parent. The child then calls exec to become a different program, and the parent calls wait to collect it. That's exactly how your time utility homework worked. Threads are lighter: they share the process's memory but each has its own stack and registers, so they can run in parallel on multiple cores, like the sorting threads in your project. But parallelism has a limit. Amdahl's law: if ten percent of your program is serial, then even with infinite cores the best speedup is one over point one, which is ten times. Speedup is capped by whatever you can't parallelize."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Race condition: why count++ is dangerous", "code": "count++  is really:   load  r1, count\n                      add   r1, r1, 1\n                      store count, r1\n\nT1: load (5)   T2: load (5)   T1: store 6   T2: store 6\n→ two increments, count = 6 instead of 7\n\nfix:  pthread_mutex_lock(&m); count++; pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);",
  "say": "Here's the bug that makes concurrency hard. Count plus plus looks like one step, but the machine does three: load, add, store. Suppose count is five, and two threads both increment it. Thread one loads five. Before it stores, thread two also loads five. Thread one stores six. Thread two stores six. Two increments happened, but count is six, not seven. That's a race condition: the answer depends on timing. The fix is a critical section. Wrap the increment in a mutex lock and unlock, so only one thread can be inside at a time. That's exactly what you did to make your stack thread safe. Be careful with multiple locks, though. If thread one holds lock A and wants B, while thread two holds B and wants A, they wait forever. That's deadlock. The simplest prevention: always acquire locks in the same global order."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "CPU scheduling", "pts": ["FCFS: simple, but short jobs wait behind long ones", "SJF: optimal average wait (needs burst estimates)", "Round Robin: time quantum q — responsive", "Example bursts 24, 3, 3:  FCFS avg wait 17 → SJF avg wait 3"],
  "say": "The scheduler decides which ready process runs next. First come first served is simple, but watch this. Three jobs arrive with bursts of twenty four, three and three milliseconds. In that order, the waits are zero, twenty four and twenty seven, an average of seventeen. Run the short jobs first, shortest job first, and the waits are zero, three and six, an average of three. Same work, a sixth of the waiting. Shortest job first is provably optimal for average wait, but you have to predict burst lengths. Round robin gives each process a time slice in turn, which keeps interactive programs responsive. Real systems mix these with multilevel feedback queues."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Virtual memory and paging", "pts": ["Address = page number + offset → page table → frame", "TLB caches recent translations", "Page fault → load from disk, maybe evict a page", "LRU ≈ good · FIFO can show Belady's anomaly · thrashing"],
  "say": "Every process thinks it has a huge private memory starting at address zero. That's virtual memory. Memory is split into fixed size pages, say four kilobytes. A virtual address splits into a page number and an offset, and the page table maps that page to a physical frame. Since looking up the table costs another memory access, the hardware caches recent translations in a T L B. If a page isn't in memory at all, you get a page fault, and the OS loads it from disk, evicting another page if memory is full. Which page to evict? Least recently used is a great approximation of optimal. Your simulation homework showed the payoff: doubling physical memory cut page faults from five hundred thirty nine to two hundred forty four. Starve a system of memory and it thrashes, spending all its time paging instead of computing."},
]},

{"code": "EGEE 401", "sem": "Fall 2019", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Engineering Economics & Ethics", "sub": "EGEE / EGCP 401 · Newnan, 14th ed.",
  "say": "Engineering Economics. Engineers don't just ask, does it work? They ask, is it worth it? This course gives you the math to answer that, plus the ethics to know when the answer shouldn't be about money at all."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Time value of money", "code": "F = P (1 + i)^n          P = F (1 + i)^−n\n\n$1,000 at 6% for 10 years:\n  F = 1000 · 1.06^10 = $1,790.85\n\nLoan payment:  A = P · i(1+i)^n / ((1+i)^n − 1)\n  $20,000 car, 6%/yr → 0.5%/mo, 60 months:\n  A = 20000 · 0.005·1.005^60 / (1.005^60 − 1) ≈ $386.66 / month",
  "say": "A dollar today is worth more than a dollar next year, because today's dollar can earn interest. Compound interest: future value equals present value times one plus i to the n. A thousand dollars at six percent for ten years grows to about one thousand seven hundred ninety one dollars. Run it backwards to discount future money to today. The factor you'll use most in real life is capital recovery, the loan payment formula. A twenty thousand dollar car loan at six percent a year is half a percent a month, over sixty months. Plug in and the payment is about three hundred eighty seven dollars a month. Always start these problems by drawing a cash flow diagram: arrows up for money in, arrows down for money out, time along the bottom."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Comparing alternatives", "pts": ["Present worth: discount everything to today at the MARR", "Annual worth: equivalent yearly cost — handles unequal lives", "IRR: the rate where PW = 0 · accept if IRR ≥ MARR", "Mutually exclusive? analyze the increment (B − A)"],
  "say": "To choose between options, put them on a common basis. Present worth discounts every cash flow back to today at your minimum attractive rate of return, and the bigger present worth wins. Annual worth converts everything to an equivalent yearly amount, which is handy when machines have different lifespans. The rate of return method finds the interest rate that makes present worth exactly zero. If that rate beats your minimum, the project's worth doing. One trap: when choosing between two projects, don't just pick the higher rate of return. Analyze the difference between them, the incremental investment, and ask whether that extra money earns enough."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Ethics: when engineering fails", "pts": ["Codes (NSPE, IEEE): public safety comes first", "Challenger: engineers' O-ring warnings overruled", "I-35W (2007): undersized gusset plates + added load", "Pattern: pressure, poor communication, no escalation"],
  "say": "The other half of the course is professionalism. Every engineering code of ethics puts the safety, health and welfare of the public first, above the employer and above the client. The case studies show why. Before the Challenger launch, engineers warned that the O rings could fail in cold weather and were overruled. Your own case study, the I thirty five W bridge in Minneapolis, collapsed in two thousand seven. Investigators traced it to gusset plates that were undersized in the original design, made worse by added weight from later renovations and construction materials parked on the deck. The pattern repeats: schedule and cost pressure, warnings that don't travel up the chain, and nobody willing to stop the line. Your job as an engineer includes speaking up."},
]},
]
